-- Vocabulary V1 step by step (IELTS-V1 v1.1.0): five guided steps and one mixed check, in the Lesson 6 format
-- (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer). Auto-marked, so filed as reading tasks; every item is
-- mapped to the vocabulary subskill it practises. Owner instruction 2026-10-28 13:01 (Asia/Bishkek): 'Go next'.
-- Named-student pilot with requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json.
do $steps$
declare art constant text := '

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

THE PASSAGE: Repair cafés

A. Over the past decade, repair cafés have appeared in a substantial number of towns and cities across Europe. At these events, which are usually held once a month in community halls or libraries, volunteers with technical skills help residents to mend household items such as lamps, bicycles, clothing and small electrical appliances. Visitors do not pay for the service, although many leave a voluntary donation towards the cost of tools and refreshments.

B. The organisers'' central argument is that a large proportion of discarded products are not genuinely beyond repair. Instead, they are thrown away because their owners lack the tools, the knowledge or simply the confidence to attempt a repair themselves. Visitors are therefore expected to stay with their item and take part in the repair rather than leave it to be collected later, on the principle that people who have fixed something once are more likely to try again at home.

C. Data collected by one national network suggest that roughly two thirds of the items brought in are successfully repaired on the day. Those that cannot be fixed are frequently electronic devices for which spare parts are no longer manufactured, or products that have been glued or sealed in a way that prevents them from being opened without being damaged. The network has used these records to argue that manufacturers should be required to make products easier to dismantle, so that worn components can be replaced rather than the whole product being discarded.

D. Surveys of visitors indicate that attendance is predominantly made up of people over the age of fifty, many of whom grew up at a time when mending clothes and appliances was a routine part of household life. Organisers are keen to attract younger residents, and several cafés now run sessions specifically for students, although it is not yet clear whether these have changed the overall age profile.

E. Funding remains a persistent difficulty. Most cafés depend on small grants from local councils, which are frequently inadequate to cover the cost of room hire and specialist equipment, and a number have consequently been forced to reduce the frequency of their events or close altogether. Volunteer numbers are also a concern, since a small number of experienced repairers often carry much of the workload, and organisers worry about what will happen when they are no longer able to take part.

F. Manufacturers'' responses have been mixed. Some have begun to publish repair manuals and sell spare parts directly to the public. Others have been reluctant to do so, arguing that untrained people could damage products or injure themselves, particularly when working with electrical items. In several countries, however, new legislation now obliges manufacturers of certain appliances to make spare parts available for a fixed number of years after a product is withdrawn from sale.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r58-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V1 step 1 of 6: Meet the words','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the meaning of each word. Do not read the passage yet.','Know the exact meaning, including how much, how sure and how long. ''Predominantly'' is most, not all; ''reluctant'' is unwilling, not refusing.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "What does ''inadequate'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  not enough"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  absolutely necessary"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  not used any more"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  lasting a short time"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "What does ''predominantly'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  only"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  about half"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  mostly, but not completely"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  hardly at all"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "What does ''negligible'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  very large"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  so small that it hardly matters"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  impossible to measure"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  growing quickly"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "What does ''interim'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  very expensive"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  not allowed by law"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  lasting only until something permanent is ready"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  permanent"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "What does ''reluctant'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  unable to do something"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  eager to do something"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  refusing completely"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  not wanting to do something"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "What does ''obsolete'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  old but still popular"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  no longer used because something newer exists"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  newly invented"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  broken and dangerous"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 6, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, ''Inadequate'' means not enough, or not good enough: ''inadequate funding'' = too little money.\n2 C, ''Predominantly'' means mainly. ''Predominantly female'' = most were women, not all.\n3 B, ''Negligible'' means tiny: ''a negligible effect'' = almost no effect.\n4 C, ''Interim'' means temporary: ''an interim measure'' is used until a permanent one is ready.\n5 D, ''Reluctant'' means unwilling. It does not mean refusing: a reluctant person may still do it.\n6 B, ''Obsolete'' means outdated: nobody uses it now because something newer exists.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Vocabulary in context: learn the exact meaning of high-frequency IELTS paraphrase words."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: same idea, different words

IELTS almost never repeats the words of the text in the question. It uses paraphrase: different words with the same meaning.
So you need to know some common sets very well:
- inadequate / insufficient = not enough
- predominantly / mainly = mostly, but not completely
- negligible / minimal = so small that it hardly matters
- interim / temporary = only until something permanent is ready
- reluctant / unwilling = not wanting to do something (but not refusing)
- obsolete / outdated = no longer used because something newer exists

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Consequently, the library extended its opening hours.''
''Consequently'' = as a result. So: because of what was said before, the library opened for longer.
Careful: it does not say WHY; it only links the result to the sentence before.

Now choose the meaning of six words.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'2b10be6b3b8373a92ed82304a69dd19d56b4598614815adab3bb83c7ec1dfd67',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r59-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V1 step 2 of 6: Exact or near-miss?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the sentence that means exactly the same as the original.','A near-miss changes one thing: how much, how sure, when, why or who. Check all five before you choose.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Original: ''The plan was substantially more expensive than expected.''", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  The plan cost a lot more than people had predicted."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  The plan will cost more than people predict."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  The plan cost less than people had predicted."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  The plan cost slightly more than people had predicted."}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Original: ''Some residents were hesitant to sign the petition.''", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Some residents did not really want to sign the petition."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  The council was hesitant to accept the petition."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Most residents refused to sign the petition."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  Some residents signed the petition eagerly."}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Original: ''Smoking is prohibited throughout the station.''", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Smoking is allowed only outside the station entrance."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Smoking is discouraged in the station."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Smoking will soon be banned in the station."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  Smoking is not allowed anywhere in the station."}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Original: ''The new bridge is likely to reduce journey times.''", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Journey times have already become shorter because of the new bridge."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Journey times will certainly become shorter because of the new bridge."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Journey times will probably become shorter because of the new bridge."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  The new bridge will probably make journeys longer."}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Original: ''In the region of 2,000 jobs were lost.''", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Exactly 2,000 jobs disappeared."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  More than 2,000 jobs disappeared."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  About 2,000 new jobs were created."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  About 2,000 jobs disappeared."}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Original: ''Volunteers acquired new skills during the project.''", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Volunteers taught new skills to other people."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Volunteers gained new abilities while working on the project."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Volunteers already had the skills before the project."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  Volunteers will gain new skills on the next project."}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, ''Substantially'' = by a large amount, and the time is past. The others change the amount, the direction or the time.\n2 A, ''Hesitant'' = not really wanting to. The others change who (most, the council) or the attitude (refused, eagerly).\n3 D, ''Prohibited'' = not allowed; ''throughout'' = everywhere. ''Discouraged'' is weaker, and ''will soon'' changes the time.\n4 C, ''Likely'' = probably, not certainly, and it is about the future. The others change certainty, time or direction.\n5 D, ''In the region of'' = about. ''Exactly'' and ''more than'' change the amount; ''created'' reverses the meaning.\n6 B, ''Acquired'' = gained. The others change when they had the skills, who learned, or the time.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Interpret lexical nuance: tell an exact paraphrase from a near-miss that changes quantity, certainty, time, cause or person."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: one small change makes it wrong

A near-miss paraphrase looks right but changes one detail. Check five things:
- HOW MUCH: ''roughly 300'' is not ''exactly 300''; ''most'' is not ''all''.
- HOW SURE: ''may'' is not ''will''.
- WHEN: past, present or future must stay the same.
- WHY: a reason that is not in the original must not be added.
- WHO: the same person or group must do the action.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Original: ''Approximately 300 people attended the meeting.''
''Exactly 300 people attended.'' -> wrong amount (approximately = about).
''About 300 people came to the meeting.'' -> exact paraphrase.

Now choose the exact paraphrase six times.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'d21e7219218121de67f58fe1ca1d89c93c105b74f074dcf7e2ff09db277e59e0',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r60-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V1 step 3 of 6: Find the word in the passage','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then find the ONE word in the named paragraph that has the meaning given. Copy it exactly.','Read only the named paragraph, look for the same meaning, and copy the word exactly as it is spelled in the passage.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Paragraph E: ''not enough'' = ________ (ONE word from the passage)", "accepted_answers": ["inadequate"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Paragraph D: ''mainly'' = ________ (ONE word from the passage)", "accepted_answers": ["predominantly"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Paragraph F: ''not wanting to do something'' = ________ (ONE word from the passage)", "accepted_answers": ["reluctant"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Paragraph C: ''approximately'' = ________ (ONE word from the passage)", "accepted_answers": ["roughly"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Paragraph E: ''as a result'' = ________ (ONE word from the passage)", "accepted_answers": ["consequently"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Paragraph A: ''large'' (describing a number) = ________ (ONE word from the passage)", "accepted_answers": ["substantial"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 inadequate: \"which are frequently inadequate to cover the cost of room hire and specialist equipment\".\n2 predominantly: \"attendance is predominantly made up of people over the age of fifty\".\n3 reluctant: \"Others have been reluctant to do so\".\n4 roughly: \"roughly two thirds of the items brought in are successfully repaired\".\n5 consequently: \"a number have consequently been forced to reduce the frequency of their events\".\n6 substantial: \"repair cafés have appeared in a substantial number of towns and cities\".\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Infer word meaning from context: find the passage word that matches a given meaning."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: find the passage''s word for the idea

In the test, the question gives you the meaning and the passage has the word, or the other way round.
1. Go to the named paragraph.
2. Look for a word with the same meaning, not the same spelling.
3. Copy it exactly. Spelling counts.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Paragraph A: which ONE word means ''not paid for, given by choice''?
Proof: "many leave a voluntary donation towards the cost of tools and refreshments".
Answer: voluntary.

Now find six words.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'5dab8492a5f5dd3dab2e722759e66d77d359b88de83d27d26c480a1c91a1382b',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r61-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V1 step 4 of 6: Spot the near-miss','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN. Check how much, how sure and when before you decide.','A statement that changes ''most'' to ''all'' or ''some'' to ''every'' is FALSE when the passage gives the real amount.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "All of the people who attend repair cafés are over fifty.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Repair cafés are generally held once a month.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Most cafés receive enough money from councils to cover their costs.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Every manufacturer now sells spare parts directly to the public.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Councils have increased their grants because of the cafés'' money problems.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Some cafés now hold fewer events because they are short of money.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 FALSE. Proof: \"attendance is predominantly made up of people over the age of fifty\". Predominantly = most, not all.\n2 TRUE. Proof: \"which are usually held once a month\". Generally = usually.\n3 FALSE. Proof: grants \"are frequently inadequate to cover the cost of room hire and specialist equipment\".\n4 FALSE. Proof: \"Some have begun to publish repair manuals and sell spare parts directly to the public. Others have been reluctant\". Some, not every.\n5 NOT GIVEN. The passage says grants are often inadequate, but nothing about councils increasing them.\n6 TRUE. Proof: \"a number have consequently been forced to reduce the frequency of their events\". Fewer events = reduce the frequency.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "True/False/Not Given with paraphrase: recognise statements that change quantity, certainty or time."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: paraphrase decides TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN

Most TRUE answers are paraphrases. Most FALSE answers are near-misses: they change one detail.
- ''predominantly'' (most) in the passage, ''all'' in the statement -> FALSE
- ''some'' in the passage, ''every'' in the statement -> FALSE
- topic in the passage but no information about the detail -> NOT GIVEN

WORKED EXAMPLE
Statement: ''Visitors are encouraged to help fix their own belongings.''
Proof: "Visitors are therefore expected to stay with their item and take part in the repair".
Take part in the repair = help fix. -> TRUE

Now check six statements.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'819374c8f6833e65b6ce1812aed469f8cfae2dd32cc35b87ab473cc069adcafb',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r62-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V1 step 5 of 6: Choose the word that fits','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the word that completes each sentence with the right meaning and grammar.','The right word fits the meaning AND the grammar of the gap. Read the words just before and after it.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "The old computers are now ______; no one makes software for them any more.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  obsolete"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  substantial"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  interim"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  reluctant"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Clean drinking water is ______ for every community.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  predominantly"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  negligible"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  vital"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  obsolete"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The rise in price was ______: just one cent.", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  substantial"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  negligible"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  vital"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  considerable"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Many people are ______ to change their bank because it takes time.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reluctant"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  inadequate"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  obsolete"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  interim"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "The station closed for repairs. ______, trains were replaced by buses.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "e75c01d6-b850-4f9e-87bb-f014b7c3947f", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Approximately"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Predominantly"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Consequently"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  However"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "An ______ head teacher will run the school until a permanent one is appointed.", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "07c8e6ee-7ec1-412a-aa64-d2f74df41a6a", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  substantial"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  obsolete"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  elderly"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  interim"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, obsolete: no longer used because something newer exists.\n2 C, vital: absolutely necessary. ''Negligible'' means tiny, and ''predominantly'' is an adverb.\n3 B, negligible: one cent is so small that it hardly matters. ''Substantial'' and ''considerable'' mean large.\n4 A, reluctant (to do): not wanting to do something.\n5 C, Consequently: as a result of the closure. ''However'' would show a contrast, but the second sentence is a result.\n6 D, interim: temporary, until a permanent head teacher is appointed.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Use precise vocabulary: choose the paraphrase word that fits a sentence''s meaning and grammar."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: meaning and grammar together

To use a new word, check two things:
1. MEANING: does it say exactly what the sentence needs?
2. GRAMMAR: does it fit the words around it? (''reluctant TO do''; ''an interim measure''; ''Consequently, ...'')

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Funding was ______ to finish the project, so work stopped.''
Meaning: not enough. Grammar: adjective + ''to''. -> inadequate
''Negligible'' is also an adjective, but it means tiny, not ''not enough for something''.

Now choose six words.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'a2eaf221e01a1bcb6f8cce98e4627572f6a01b88954d3ba7e914c5018a668d3b',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r63-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V1 step 6 of 6: Mixed check','reading','independent_check','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then answer six new questions on the passage. Work on your own, without notes, a dictionary or help. Tell your teacher if anything interrupted you.','Match meaning, not words: check how much, how sure, when and who, and use the words around a phrase to work out what it means.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Visitors must pay for the tools used to repair their items.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "The repair network wants manufacturers to make products easier to take apart.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Younger visitors repair items more successfully than older ones.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "In paragraph B, ''genuinely beyond repair'' means ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "e55dff22-7ea2-4bb6-b724-7d129b554bb4", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  only slightly damaged"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  recently repaired"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  really impossible to fix"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  too expensive to buy"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "In paragraph E, ''carry much of the workload'' means the experienced repairers ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "e55dff22-7ea2-4bb6-b724-7d129b554bb4", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  train new volunteers"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  do a large part of the work"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  carry heavy equipment"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  earn the most money"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "In paragraph F, ''obliges'' is closest in meaning to ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "e3155f38-aea7-4267-a0c3-44273b1d7d14", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  pays"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  requires"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  prevents"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  encourages"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 FALSE. Proof: \"Visitors do not pay for the service\". Donations are voluntary.\n2 TRUE. Proof: \"manufacturers should be required to make products easier to dismantle\". Take apart = dismantle.\n3 NOT GIVEN. The passage describes the age of visitors, but never compares how successful different ages are.\n4 C, really impossible to fix: organisers argue many discarded products \"are not genuinely beyond repair\", so they could be fixed.\n5 B, do a large part of the work: \"a small number of experienced repairers often carry much of the workload\".\n6 B, requires: new legislation \"obliges manufacturers ... to make spare parts available\". A law that obliges you makes you do it.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Paraphrase recognition on one passage: TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN and word meaning in context."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the whole routine

1. Find the part of the passage the question is about.
2. Match the MEANING: the passage and the question will use different words.
3. Check the details: how much, how sure, when, who.
4. For a phrase, read the sentence around it and choose the meaning that fits there.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Question: In paragraph C, what does ''dismantle'' mean?
Context: products should be easier to dismantle "so that worn components can be replaced".
To replace parts you must take the product apart. -> dismantle = take apart

Now answer six new questions on your own.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'63d85e157ded00f7db50f407210c3c1b61b8a7be446ffab0ae2ab304372df8e2',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
end $steps$;
